# <%= current_page.data.title %>

There is no prescribed cadence for updates to DataHub, but new minor versions bumps are typically released every couple of months.
Due to the rapid pace of feature development within the platform we aim to be close to the most recent release (without unnecessary exposure to unintended breaking changes).

An upgrade has two parts, and both need doing:

* **Server**: the DataHub Helm chart and image version, deployed from [data-catalogue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-catalogue). Schema and index changes are handled by a [dedicated upgrade job](https://github.com/acryldata/datahub-helm/tree/master/charts/datahub/templates/datahub-upgrade).
* **Client**: the `acryl-datahub` Python package used by our ingestion, and the managed ingestion CLI version. These should match the server version.

## Before you start

1. Check the [DataHub 'Updating DataHub' page for breaking changes](https://datahubproject.io/docs/how/updating-datahub#breaking-changes) in every release between the current version and the destination version.
2. Check the DataHub Helm releases for the [latest chart release that's compatible with the destination version](https://github.com/acryldata/datahub-helm/blame/master/charts/datahub/Chart.yaml#L10). A new DataHub version may need a new chart version line.
3. Compare the [DataHub Helm chart changes from the current version to the new version](https://github.com/acryldata/datahub-helm/compare/datahub-0.3.30...datahub-0.4.6) (set 'base' and 'compare' to the correct releases). Look for:
    * new values that need adding to our values files
    * changed defaults that need overriding to keep our current behaviour
4. Check the steps in `.github/workflows/deploy-workflow.yml` marked "Remove this step once DataHub ships a fix upstream". If the new release fixes the issue, remove the step as part of the upgrade.

## Upgrade dev

Dev is upgraded first and runs ahead of preprod and prod until testing is complete.

1. In `.github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml`, update `datahub_helm_version` (and `datahub_prereqs_helm_version` if needed).
2. In `helm_deploy/dev/values-base.yaml`, set `global.datahub.version` and `global.datahub.managed_ingestion.defaultCliVersion` to the new versions.
3. In `pyproject.toml`, update `acryl-datahub[dbt, glue, postgres]` to match the server version, run `uv lock`, and run the Python tests. Dependabot may already have raised this PR.
4. Raise the upgrade PR and send an 'alert' message to the [#data-catalogue-dev](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C06UQ5HSR25) channel to let others know that DataHub may be disrupted.

You do not need to set a version in `helm_deploy/mae-consumer-timeseries-values.yaml`. It inherits the version from the environment values so it always matches GMS.

## Test in dev

1. Deploy to dev and test the [DataHub interface](https://datahub-catalogue-dev.apps.live.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/).
2. Check that [Find MoJ Data dev](https://dev.find-moj-data.service.justice.gov.uk/) still works by [running a test deployment](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/find-moj-data/actions/workflows/test-and-deploy.yml).
    * Test multiple pages of Find MoJ Data. Changes not described as breaking have broken our front end before.
3. Run a full ingestion in dev, including CaDeT, and check it completes. New client versions can change how ingestion talks to GMS (see [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)).
4. Run the post-ingestion checks workflow (`post-ingestion-checks.yml`).
5. If there are issues with the upgrade, work with the team to decide the best course of action. This may involve a temporary rollback.

## Upgrade preprod and prod

Once there are no issues in dev:

1. Move `global.datahub.version` and `defaultCliVersion` from `helm_deploy/dev/values-base.yaml` to `helm_deploy/values-base.yaml`.
2. Update `datahub_helm_version` for both environments in `.github/workflows/deploy-staged.yml`.
3. Merge the PR and roll out to preprod, then prod. Run an ingestion and check Find MoJ Data in each environment.

## Troubleshooting

### Another operation in progress

If helm fails with `Error: UPGRADE FAILED: another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress`, you can run helm locally to rollback Datahub to an earlier revision, and then rerun the job.

To see the revision numbers:

```sh
helm history datahub --namespace data-platform-datahub-catalogue-dev
```

To rollback to a specific revision:

```sh
helm rollback datahub $REVISION --namespace data-platform-datahub-catalogue-dev
```

### Pre-upgrade hooks failed

If you get an error like this

```sh
Error: UPGRADE FAILED: release datahub failed, and has been rolled back due to atomic being set: pre-upgrade hooks failed: 1 error occurred:
  * timed out waiting for the condition
```

It means one of the jobs that run via pre-upgrade hooks failed. You should be able to see which one was running last from the github actions output. You can then use `k9s` to check the logs for that job.

This error can be caused by:

* a misconfigured ([or missing](https://github.com/datahub-project/datahub/issues/11655)) docker image tag
* a changed chart default, for example chart 1.1.x enabled zero-downtime upgrades by default, so we set `global.datahub.systemUpdate.zdu.enable: false` in `helm_deploy/values-base.yaml`

### Ingestion gets 403 errors

The WAF (ModSecurity on `datahub-frontend`) is blocking a request the new client makes.

For example, the 1.7 dbt source sends batches of dataset URNs to `/api/gms/openapi/v3/entity/dataset/batchGet`, and some URNs contain strings the WAF treats as attacks.

To fix this, find the rule ID in the ingress logs and add a `SecRule REQUEST_URI ... ctl:ruleRemoveById=...` exclusion to the `datahub-frontend` ModSecurity snippet in `helm_deploy/values-base.yaml`, using the next free `id`. Keep the exclusion limited to the affected path.

### Custom ingestion source fails

Our custom sources extend DataHub classes, so a client upgrade (even a patch version) can remove or rename methods we override. This usually shows up as an `AttributeError` or failing tests.

For example, 1.7.0.16 replaced `DBTCoreSource.loadManifestAndCatalog()` with `load_projects()`, so `ingestion/cadet_dbt_source/source.py` had to be updated.

To fix this, compare the DataHub source code between the old and new versions and update our override.

### CaDeT ingestion times out

CaDeT ingestion can slow down after an upgrade. Increase `timeout-minutes` in `.github/workflows/ingest-cadet-metadata.yml` (it was raised from 240 to 360 for 1.7).

The AWS role session (`role-duration-seconds`) can't be raised past the role's 4 hour maximum. This is fine, as AWS is only used at the start of the run.
